<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span><span id="S4.T3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impact of masking strategy and ratio.</span> Performance comparison of <span id="S4.T3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PAPO</span> using different approaches for constructing <math id="S4.T3.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\text{mask}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mtext>mask</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\text{mask}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Despite its simplicity, random masking empirically outperforms semantic-aware masking.
A sufficiently large masking ratio (e.g., 0.6 or 0.8) yields stronger performance, while ratios that are too low (e.g., 0.4) or too high (e.g., 1.0) are less effective. See detailed discussion in §<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Ablation on Key Design Choices ‣ 5 Results ‣ Perception-Aware Policy Optimization for Multimodal Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
